• Step 1: Start
Begin the program execution.
• Step 2: Initialize Base Network
Take the initial number of computers and cable connections as input, validate them, and build the starting adjacency list (same as the original code setup).
• Step 3: Start Menu Loop
Start a do-while loop to display a continuous console menu with the following 6 options:
	1. Display Computer Network
	2. Check Direct Connection
	3. Add New Computer and Connections (Mod A)
	4. Remove Failed Cable (Mod B)
	5. Count Direct Connections (Mod C)
	6. Exit
• Step 4: Read User Choice
Accept the selected menu option (choice) from the user and process it using a switch-case block.
• Step 5: Modification A - Add New Computer (Case 3)
	• Increment the total computer count by 1 (n++).
	• Push a new empty vector slot into the adjacency list for this new node.
	• Accept the total number of links to establish for this new computer.
	• Run a loop to input target computer IDs and establish bidirectional cable connections with them.
• Step 6: Modification B - Remove Failed Cable (Case 4)
	• Accept the two computer IDs connected by the faulty network cable.
	• Search for each computer ID inside the other's respective adjacency list using std::find.
	• If the active link is found, erase the connection from both lists and print a success message.
• Step 7: Modification C - Count Direct Connections (Case 5)
	• Execute a sequential loop from computer ID 1 to n.
	• Fetch the size of each vector (adj[i].size()).
	• Display this size as the total count of direct point-to-point connections for each specific computer.
• Step 8: Handle Termination (Case 6)
	• If the user enters choice 6, display an "Exiting program..." message and break out of the loop.
• Step 9: Loop Condition
	• Repeat the entire process from Step 3 as long as the user choice is not equal to 6.
• Step 10: Stop
End the program execution.
